      "text": "[14] , which increases the complexity and cost of subsequent treatment. Foam fractionation is a separation technology based on interfacial adsorption that selectively removes solutes according to their affinity for gas \u2013 liquid interfaces. It has shown promise in PFAS-contaminated water treatment due to its simple operation, high efficiency, and relatively low cost [15] . The process exploits the amphiphilic nature of PFAS, enabling their preferential accumulation at gas \u2013 liquid interfaces and subsequent separation via bubble flotation. However, PFAS concentrations in contaminated water typically range from ng \u22c5 L \u0000 1 to mg \u22c5 L \u0000 1 , far below the critical micelle concentration. Under such conditions, conventional foam fractionation suffers from low adsorption efficiency and poor foam stability, which hinder effective separation [16,17] . Introducing sur - factants can promote foam generation and enhance PFAS migration to interfaces. In particular, the addition of cationic surfactants enables electrostatic and hydrophobic interactions with PFAS, leading to the formation of surface-active ion pairs that are more readily adsorbed onto bubbles and removed with the foamate [18] . Buckley et al. [19] ach - ieved ideal perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) removal efficiency by adding cationic surfactant CTAB with foaming durations of 20 \u2013 30 min. How - ever, conventional surfactants like CTAB exhibit high toxicity (EC 50 = 0.03 mg \u22c5 L"
